 { Welcome to Calculation: A Complete Guide for Tournament Players! You must have heard of the adage, “Give a man a fish and you feed him for a day; teach a man to fish and you feed him for a lifetime”. This course offers you both the fish and the tools for fishing. I will show you the most watertight and efficient calculation techniques which are best suited for classical chess games and puzzles. You will also learn to internalise new chess patterns and decode endgame manuals on your own. Ultimately, I want you to improve your learning curve even well after you have completed this course. The Origin of the CourseLet me share the story of how this course came about. When I first started playing chess competitively some years ago, I realised that the biggest determining factor of chess rating lies in calculation skills. Before long, I found myself devoting most of my time to solving chess puzzles, and soon became obsessed with the drill. At the height of my compulsion, I completed over 300 puzzles in a single day. Other types of chess training such as reading books and playing games took a back seat. However, even after solving chess puzzles daily for a year, something was not working out. I may have had days where my rating prospered, but it felt undeserved. Even for puzzles solved correctly, I was not ready to bet on my answers before playing them out. When I got a puzzle wrong, I barely spent any time analysing the solution. Instead, I was in a hurry to move on to the next puzzle, hoping to redeem myself from my earlier folly. This resulted in countless streaks of mistakes where I missed even the very elementary tactics. On several occasions, this drove me to utter frustration and I reset my puzzle history just to bury my shame. My poor learning attitude was costly. Despite having solved well over 20,000 puzzles in that year, my improvements failed to reflect my painstaking effort. This rude awakening propelled me to look for a good calculation system which offers a step-by-step guide to approaching sharp positions. Unfortunately, after exploring many books and videos, I could not find anything of the sort. Sure, I found some fragmented pieces of advice. For example, “Always calculate checks first” and “Loose pieces drop off”. But on their own, they provided little help. I knew if I wanted to improve, I had to develop my own system. I started keeping a “Puzzle Log” which tracked incorrectly solved puzzles. Each puzzle was labelled with the cause of my mistake such as “poor visualisation” and “incorrect evaluation”. Two weeks into this meticulous record-keeping, I noticed the Puzzle Log was overwhelmed with the label “temporary blindness”. Clearly, my impaired vision was not so temporary. So what was the remedy to blindness? It certainly could not be practice and more practice, given the inordinate amount of time I had already spent on puzzles. It was time to switch up my approach. I revisited each puzzle I got wrong and analysed it thoroughly with a singular vision in mind. I saw myself giving a chess lecture to an imaginary lower-rated audience, providing hints to solve the puzzle. Trying to become Captain Hindsight has not only sharpened my understanding of old puzzles, but somehow improved my ability to solve new ones. I got better at developing fresh insights into more positions and began compiling them systemically. A skeletal structure of this curriculum on calculation techniques was thus created. In the ensuing years, I personally tested and refined the materials iteratively, eventually teaching to numerous students, many of whom reaped significant rating increases. And now it is my pleasure to share it with you. How to Maximise ResultsThis course opens a new way of viewing the 64 squares, and I want to ensure that you experience the same substantial improvements that my students and I have enjoyed. Hence, I am going to provide three guidelines that will maximise your results. 1. Go through the course sequentially. I have carefully crafted the course such that each chapter builds on the knowledge covered in previous ones. It is therefore important to go through the course in the order that is presented. Otherwise, you may not understand some of the concepts as deeply as you should. 2. Pay close attention to the informational pages The informational pages are where I reveal both the execution and the rationale of the techniques. By having a greater understanding of the techniques, you will be able to utilise them more tactfully and efficiently. 3. Execute the techniques conscientiously. When you see a new puzzle, you may be tempted to slip back to your old techniques. Succumbing to this knee-jerk reaction will be counterproductive. Instead, I urge you to closely rely on what you have learnt from this course, only playing out the moves after checking through all the variations. To facilitate this, I have set the default time for each puzzle to ‘Unlimited’. ContentsThe first chapter introduces The Burger Technique. This is the foundation on which we will build in the subsequent chapters. The second is on Reciprocal Thinking. It explains how you can swiftly uncover the best candidate move by dredging out all the bad ones. Following that, in the third chapter, we will learn how to internalise new chess patterns effectively using a four-step guide: Pattern Internalisation Essentials. The fourth chapter covers Essential Theoretical Endgames. Its main focus is to help you connect the dots in endgame manuals. Next, in the penultimate chapter, we will deal with important Endgame Ideas that are commonly overlooked. Finally, the course concludes with remedies to three Typical Calculation Mistakes. } 1. -- 
